{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>The first of the first fruits of your land.</b> The first fruits are forbidden for benefit, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded that this is why the commandment of first fruits was juxtaposed to the commandment 'You shall not cook a kid,' to tell you: just as meat in milk is forbidden for benefit, so too first fruits are forbidden for benefit. And they further expounded of it that one may not bring first fruits except from ripened produce, not from unripe (semadar)." "<b>You shall not cook a kid in its mother's milk.</b> This verse appears three times in the Torah, and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: one for the prohibition of eating, one for the prohibition of benefit, and one for the prohibition of cooking. And the reason the verse expressed it in the language of cooking is that the Torah forbade it by way of cooking, and one incites lashes from the Torah for its cooking and likewise for its eating by way of cooking; and anything not by way of cooking is not forbidden from the Torah but only rabbinically — and so too is the view of my master of blessed memory. And the reason it did not say 'You shall not eat' is that the prohibition of benefit is included in it, for they said: everywhere where 'You shall not eat' is stated, 'You shall not eat' implies both the prohibition of eating and the prohibition of benefit, unless the verse specifies otherwise. For had it said 'You shall not eat,' I would have understood that it is like the other prohibitions, for which there is no liability for eating them except by way of their normal benefit, but not in a manner that is not the way of their benefit — one would be exempt. Therefore the verse did not express it in the language of eating, to teach you that if one ate it not by way of its benefit, for example if one swallowed it hot and it burned his throat, he is liable to lashes from the Torah — which is not the case with other prohibitions. And since it did not mention the language of eating, therefore a third verse was needed for the prohibition of benefit. But in any case one incites lashes only for its cooking and for its eating, even not by way of its benefit. And so you find in tractate Pesahim chapter 2, where they said: 'Meat in milk — for this reason the Merciful One did not write \"eating\" concerning it itself, to teach that one incites lashes for it even not by way of its benefit.' And there are only two commandments for which a person incites lashes for eating them even not by way of their benefit: meat in milk and mixed seeds in a vineyard (kil'ei ha-kerem). And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said: Everyone agrees concerning kil'ei ha-kerem that one incites lashes for them even not by way of their benefit. What is the reason? Because 'eating' is not written concerning them, but rather (Deuteronomy 22:9): ״פן תקדש״, and we expound: ״פן תוקד אש״." "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the reason for this commandment is that it dulls the heart, for the fat is made from the blood, and the blood is of bad temperament and produces cruelty. And one of the reasons for its prohibition is that it does not undergo change and alteration in the body like other eaten things, and therefore its bad nature remains within it without change; and even though it has now changed from blood to milk and undergone change and alteration in being transformed into something else — nonetheless, when one goes back and mixes it with the meat, it reverts to the potency of blood and its original nature as at first, and their admixture together dulls the heart and produces coarseness and an evil disposition in the soul of the eater." "<b>And likewise</b> is the view of the physicians concerning a mixture of fish and cheese that were cooked together, that it produces an evil disposition and the disease of leprosy (tzara'at). And the reason this commandment is always mentioned alongside the festivals is to warn Israel, who ascend three times a year for the pilgrimage to the place of prophecy, not to dull the heart with forbidden foods, but rather that their matter be pure and clear to contemplate the ways of the Torah, and that their heart be prepared for the attainment of knowledge of Him, blessed be He." "<b>And the Rambam</b> of blessed memory wrote a reason for this in his reasons of the commandments: because it was the practice of idol worshippers that they would eat meat in milk in the house of their abominations on the days of their festivals; therefore the Torah came to forbid it. And the verse says: when you come three times a year to the house of YHWH your Elohim, 'You shall not cook a kid in its mother's milk' — like the practice of idol worshippers who do so. And this is why this verse is mentioned in two places in the context of the festivals, for so is the way of the Torah: to forbid the things that belong to idols and to command us to do the opposite, in order to uproot the root of idolatry from the world. This is what the Rav of blessed memory wrote as the reason for this commandment, and although this is not his language, it is his intent." "<b>And from</b> what appears, these reasons are insufficient and are not the essential reason for the commandments, but only to turn away the questioner. For this commandment is among the ḥuqqim; it belongs to the class of the red heifer and the scapegoat, and so did the Sages, of blessed memory, expound: in the time to come the Holy One, blessed be He, will reveal to Israel why the Torah commanded concerning meat in milk and the red heifer and the scapegoat. And since it is so, the view of the Sages is that this commandment is among the hidden secrets of the Torah, for surely in the time to come there will be neither dullness of heart nor idolatry, since we are assured by the promise (Deuteronomy 30:6): ״ומל ה׳ אלהיך״ — 'And HaShem your Elohim will circumcise,' etc., and likewise by the promise (Zephaniah 3:9): ״כי אז אהפוך אל עמים שפה ברורה״ — 'For then I will turn the peoples to a pure language,' etc. But this commandment has a secret." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah,</b> you already know that all the commandments, every one, are divine, and each one of them is depicted after the forms of the supernal realities, and that the ḥuqqim are a remembrance and likeness of above; for therefore they are called ḥuqqim because they are engraved on high. And the meat and the milk are a likeness and an allusion corresponding to certain supernal realities; and just as meat and milk are each permitted on its own, so too those supernal realities are each distinct on its own; and just as the essence of the prohibition is only the combination, and the Torah distanced it and decreed a separation and distance between them, so too those realities we must understand have a distinction between them. And one must not equate the holiness of above with the holiness of below, even though all is unified one with the other. And in this world, in which the evil inclination exists, we need to unify them, and we unify and declare that He and His name are one. Therefore when the Sages, of blessed memory, said that in the time to come the Holy One, blessed be He, will reveal to Israel the reason for the prohibition of meat in milk, their intention in this statement is that although it is a commandment imposed upon us, governing our food and sustenance in this physical world, this world is not worthy, and it is not fitting, for the reason and the secret to be revealed in it — because this world contains the evil inclination, and the minds of creatures would not bear it; perhaps they would think concerning HaShem and entertain thoughts of two powers, heaven forbid. But in the time to come, at the time of the abolition of the evil inclination, which is the time of the resurrection of the dead, there all will understand the secret from its root, and there understanding and apprehension will increase, and we will be able to distinguish and recognize that He is one and His name is one, as the prophet says (Zechariah 14:9): ״ביום ההוא יהיה ה׳ אחד ושמו אחד״ — 'On that day HaShem will be one and His name one'; and likewise at that time (Isaiah 52:6): ״לכן ידע עמי שמי״ — 'Therefore My people shall know My name.' And this is the revelation of the reason by the Holy One, blessed be He, because the revelation of the reason and the secret is not in this world but only at that time; for then the prohibition of meat and milk will be abolished. And from this you should be roused to understand why the angels ate meat with milk, as it says (Genesis 18:8): ״ויקח חמאה וחלב ובן הבקר״ — 'And he took curd and milk and the calf,' for the angels have no evil inclination — and even among physical human beings this commandment will not apply at the time of the abolition of the evil inclination. And therefore the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded (Leviticus 19:3): ״את חקותי תשמרו״ — 'You shall keep My ḥuqqim,' etc. — 'I am HaShem': I engraved them, and you have no permission to speculate about them — that is, I engraved them on high, and you have no permission to speculate about them, for speculation about the reason of the ḥuqqim such as this is harmful in this world, because of the evil inclination that leads a person astray. And according to its plain sense one may say that ״ביום ההוא יהיה ה׳ אחד ושמו אחד״ — 'On that day HaShem will be one and His name one' — concerns worship: that all will worship only the Holy One, blessed be He, and this will be one in everyone's mouth, and 'His name one' concerns the calling upon Him: that He will not be called with אל״ף דל״ת as He is called in this world, but with יו״ד ה״א as it is written."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {1 "Rabbeinu Bahya cites the well-known pun on Deuteronomy 22:9: פן תקדש read as פן תוקד אש — 'lest it be kindled with fire.' The verse citation is normalized from the Hebrew form דברים כ״ב:ט׳."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:52.091191854Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}